---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { href } from "../lib";
---

<Base
  title="Architecture review"
  description="A fixed-scope review of your delivery pipeline and cloud access, with findings ranked by risk and a practical plan to fix them."
>
  <h1>Architecture review</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A clear view of how code reaches production in your organisation, where it could be tampered with,
    and what to fix first.
  </p>

  <h2>What we look at</h2>
  <ul class="ticks">
    <li>Source control: branch and tag protection, review rules, who can bypass them.</li>
    <li>CI/CD: workflow permissions, triggers that run untrusted code, pinned dependencies, self-hosted runners.</li>
    <li>Secrets: where they live, who and what can read them, how they are rotated.</li>
    <li>Supply chain: dependency and container scanning, build provenance, what gets deployed and how it is verified.</li>
    <li>Cloud access from CI: long-lived keys, OIDC trust policies and how tightly they are scoped.</li>
    <li>AI tools in the pipeline: what they can read, write and spend.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <ul class="ticks">
    <li>A written report with each finding rated blocking, should-fix or low, and the evidence behind it.</li>
    <li>A prioritised plan your team can work through, with example fixes where they help.</li>
    <li>A walkthrough session to go through the findings and answer questions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it runs</h2>
  <p>
    We agree the scope in writing first. We work from read-only access wherever possible and never need
    production credentials. The review ends with the report and the walkthrough. If you then want help
    with the fixes, that is a separate, separately scoped piece of work.
  </p>
  <p><a class="button" href={href("contact/")}>Discuss a review</a></p>
</Base>
